%[text] %[text:anchor:T_DAB] # Design and Analyze a Dual-Active-Bridge (DAB) Converter
%[text] A dual-active-bridge (DAB) converter is an isolated bidirectional DC-DC topology in which two full bridges exchange power through a transformer and a series inductance. This design solution shows how to configure a DAB converter at a chosen operating point and analyze its closed-loop performance. The model uses the DAB plant and controller blocks from `ConverterLibrary`. The controller uses single-phase-shift modulation and regulates either the output current or the output voltage. You simulate the converter, plot the voltage and current (IV) waveforms, and estimate power loss and efficiency.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_scope] ## Design Solution Scope
%[text] In this design solution, you can:
%[text] - Configure the closed-loop DAB plant and controller at the operating point
%[text] - Simulate and inspect the DC voltage and current and the bridge current (IV) waveforms
%[text] - Estimate power loss and efficiency \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_sys] ## System Overview
%[text] The closed-loop model comprises the DAB Converter plant and the DAB Controller. The controller can regulate either the output DC voltage or the output DC current. To select constant-current mode, set the CCCV signal to one. For constant-voltage mode, set it to zero. The controller outputs the phase-shifted gate signals for the primary and secondary full bridges.
%[text] ![](text:image:55fc)
modelName = "DualActiveBridgeConverter";
open_system(modelName);
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_72e5] ## Select DAB Converter Parameters
%[text] To design the DAB converter, use the `EstimateDABConverterParameters` live script. In this workflow, you specify your design requirements and configure the power circuit; the live script plots the power-transfer curve and soft-switching regions.
%[text] To open the parameter estimation workflow, enter this command in the MATLAB® Command Window:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] edit 'EstimateDABConverterParameters'
%[text] ```
%[text] Load the default parameters and set the model name.
if ~exist('DABDesign','var')
    DABDefaultInputParameters;
end
disp(table(designTable,'VariableNames',"DAB Converter Estimated Design Parameters")); %[output:2ac23c2b]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_selectcomp] ## Select Converter Components
%[text] This design solution helps you select the ratings of the primary and secondary bridge MOSFETs and pick catalog parts that meet them. The parts come from the Simscape™ parts feature.
%[text] To open the component selection workflow, run:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] edit 'SelectDABConverterComponents'
%[text] ```
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_op] ## Define the Operating Point
%[text] Set the operating point for the waveform and loss analyses. The DAB Controller regulates either the output voltage (constant-voltage, CV) or the output current (constant-current, CC).
controlMode      = "voltage";                  % "voltage" (CV) or "current" (CC)
inputDCVoltage   = simscape.Value(770,"V");    % Primary DC input voltage (Vin)
voltageReference = simscape.Value(530,"V");    % Output-voltage setpoint, used in CV mode (Vref)
currentReference = simscape.Value(18,"A");     % Output-current setpoint, used in CC mode (Iref)
outputLoadPower  = simscape.Value(8000,"W");   % Delivered output power (Pload)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_plot] ### Plot Simulation Result
%[text] To plot the converter waveforms at ideal device fidelity, use `getConverterWaveforms`. It plots the primary and secondary bridge currents, the output current, the output voltage and the output power. To set the number of switching cycles plotted at the end of the simulation, use the `FocusCycles` parameter.
  %[control:button:3975]{"position":[1,2]}
dabIVData = getConverterWaveforms(modelName, Converter="DAB", ... %[output:group:3906c50c] %[output:85040174]
    ControlMode=controlMode, Vin=inputDCVoltage, ... %[output:85040174]
    Vref=voltageReference, Iref=currentReference, Pload=outputLoadPower, ... %[output:85040174]
    SimTime=simscape.Value(0.010,"s"), ... %[output:85040174]
    FrequencyHz=DABDesign.switchingFrequency.value, FocusCycles=8); %[output:group:3906c50c] %[output:85040174]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_loss] ### Estimate Power Loss and Efficiency
%[text] To estimate the switching and conduction losses and the efficiency, use `getConverterPowerLoss`. The bar chart shows the loss in each device and the pie chart shows the loss distribution and the efficiency.
%[text] The losses are estimated at the junction temperature set below.
    simulationTemperature = simscape.Value(25,"degC");   % Device junction temperature for the loss estimation
    lossData = getConverterPowerLoss(modelName, Converter="DAB", ... %[output:group:1e01f581] %[output:3f698eed] %[output:1746a21d]
        ControlMode=controlMode, Vin=inputDCVoltage, ... %[output:3f698eed] %[output:1746a21d]
        Vref=voltageReference, Iref=currentReference, Pload=outputLoadPower, ... %[output:3f698eed] %[output:1746a21d]
        SimTime=simscape.Value(0.010,"s"), SwitchingLossDetail="lumped", ... %[output:3f698eed] %[output:1746a21d]
        SimulationTemperature=simulationTemperature, ... %[output:3f698eed] %[output:1746a21d]
        FrequencyHz=DABDesign.switchingFrequency.value); %[output:group:1e01f581] %[output:3f698eed] %[output:1746a21d]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_concl] ## Conclusion
%[text] This design solution shows how to use MATLAB® scripting and Simscape Electrical™ to design a dual-active-bridge converter. It sizes the key passive components, models the circuit at low fidelity for fast simulation and control design, and models it in enough detail to predict losses and efficiency.
